  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1250 PM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nWednesday night through Tuesday:\n\nThe upper low will continue southward Wednesday night, reaching\nArkansas by Thursday morning per the bulk of 12Z guidance. The\nlow will then head head eastward Thursday, lifting northeast\ninto the Ohio River Valley Thursday night, deepening through\nthis period. Surface low pressure is expected to continue to\ndeepen in response to the upper level system, as it gets pulled\nnorthward around the upper low. Latest guidance is coming into\nbetter agreement in keeping the surface low far enough to the\nsoutheast as it passes by to limit precip amounts across \nsouthern Wisconsin. \n\nIt does still look like there could be some light precip \nWednesday night into early Thursday as deeper moisture and warm\nair advection wrap around the mid/upper low into southern \nWisconsin. With the core of the colder airmass still in the \narea, could see some rain/snow mix or a brief period of all \nsnow with this first round of precip, mainly from Madison \nsouthwestward. Little to no accumulation is expected (grassy \nareas at best) given the light rates and mild ground. \n\nThe next round of potential precip (likely mainly rain) would \nbe later in the day on Thanksgiving into the night as the \nsurface low passes by well to the southeast. Overall model \ntrends (including for Wednesday night) have been farther \nsoutheast with this system and thus drier for southern \nWisconsin. There are a couple 12Z solutions that keep the \nforecast area dry through the duration of this system, so a \nreduction in precip chances is possible going forward if this \ntrend continues. \n\nThe forecast is trending dry for Friday given the latest\nguidance, though kept some precip chances for now as the low\nexits the region. The door will stay open for shortwaves to move\nthrough the area at times over the weekend through early next\nweek. The bulk of model guidance is showing a system or two\nbrining some precip during this period, though there is a large\nspread in the timing and strength of any systems among 12Z\nmodels. Overall, it looks like temperatures will be above normal\nlate week into the weekend, with cooler temperatures possibly\nreturning early next week.\n\nDDV",
